-- FLOAT ARITHMETIC end to end (plan 0.75 F4). Expected exit code 23
--
-- `1.5 - 2.1` was `binop left: Type mismatch: expected Int but got Float` until
-- F4. This program mixes all three operations, so recognition lifts the whole
-- expression into ONE `arith.block.<digest>` whose body is float instructions:
-- `addsd`/`mulsd`/`subsd` on x86, `fadd.d`/`fmul.d`/`fsub.d` on RISC-V.
--
-- WHAT IT CHECKS HERE is that the program builds and reaches its exit code on
-- all three targets — i.e. the block links, the instructions assemble, and
-- nothing traps. The VALUE was checked separately and more sharply: the
-- emitted block was called from a C harness on x86-64 and from a freestanding
-- binary under qemu on riscv64, and both returned `0x4016000000000000` — bit
-- identical to what the host's own C compiler computes for the same
-- expression.
--
-- WHY THE VALUE IS NOT CHECKED THROUGH THE TRACE, which would be better:
-- `compose emitF@E <expression>` needs the expression at `Unit ω→ Float`, and
-- the value-lift realm `⊢ᵍ` covers literals, not computations. That is a
-- PRE-EXISTING limitation and not a float one — `compose emit@E (1 + 2)` fails
-- identically for `Int`. Lifting it would let `FloatEmitSpec` read arithmetic
-- results back out of the trace, which is the right home for that check.

import I.Linux.Syscalls as S
import I.Test.Emit as E

emitLit : IO Unit
emitLit = emitF@E (1.5 + 2.25 * 2.0 - 0.5)

main : IO Unit
main = compose exit@S (compose 23 emitLit)
